<section class="section wrap problem" aria-labelledby="problem-h">
  <div>
    <h2 id="problem-h">The paperwork shouldn't be the hardest part.</h2>
    <p>A Medicaid application can mean five years of statements for every account a client has ever had, arriving as shoeboxes, envelopes, email attachments and half-remembered online logins. Your staff scan it, rename it, file it, chase what's missing, and email it out again, usually against a deadline. One missing month can mean a denial for “failure to verify.”</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="ruled">
    <li><b>Paper keeps coming.</b> Clients and families bring what they have, in whatever order they find it.</li>
    <li><b>Gaps are hard to see.</b> Knowing which months are missing for which account means checking page by page.</li>
    <li><b>Sending is risky.</b> Email attachments of bank statements and IDs travel with no record of who opened them.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
  <div class="wrap section-head">
    <h2 id="how-h">Three steps from paper to proof.</h2>
    <p>Open a matter, choose its type, such as Medicaid planning, and DossierIQ sets up the folders. Then scan, check and send.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap feat">
    <div>
      <p class="step">1. Scan and upload</p>
      <h3>It reads the boxes so your paralegal doesn't have to.</h3>
      <p>Scan a stack of paper on your office PC. DossierIQ Scan splits it into individual documents, names each one, and files it in the matter's folder in your firm's own Google Drive or SharePoint. You can also upload files and email exports. Anything uncertain is flagged for your review.</p>
    </div>
